Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
when you're a kid imagination is  limitless and inspiration can come from  anywhere  so when seven-year-old jeremy rowan had  to choose a costume for  dress as your idol day at school he knew  exactly who he wanted to be  this is jeremy and this is who he chose  to dress up as  poet amanda gorman who he says recites  poetry  like it's a song she is america's first  youth poet laureate  like millions of people jeremy was  mesmerized by her grace her dignity and  her words  at the inauguration of u.s president joe  biden  when day comes we step out of the shade  a flame and unafraid the new dawn blooms  as we free it for there was always light  if only were brave enough to see it if  only  were brave enough to be it  she stepped into that moment of history  and took people's breath away  there is a renaissance in poetry right  now and it's not just driven by gorman  there are plenty of emerging eloquent  poets including here in canada  whose voices are rising robin gill has  the story  of some of them i was in ocean once  bare shoulders and whale song in my rib  cage i watched  as the rigs drilled holes in my body  they left no part of my trenches  unmanned  poetry has always been a form of art but  rarely a career  turned me into a war instead of a  homecoming  i should have evaporated sooner and yet  nisha patel has managed to make this  a full-time gig it's definitely not the  sexiest job to dream of when you're you  know 10 years old  other jobs seem more important seem  bigger seem  you know wider in their scope seem like  they're going to reach more people  but to dream of being an artist in the  literary sense is very different  this place is a love song a changing  season's memoir  a passage of time captured in the  buildings  and the businessman's dreams the 28 year  old's dream came true when edmonton  picked her as the city's poet laureate  there's a level of recognition  that comes into being a poet laureate  and so a lot of your words  are not taken you know lightly you're  able to occupy a space that  the public is able to see like a lot of  young poets  she taught herself by reading it that's  how toronto's  andrea josek got her start uber driver  jaw opens  a boa constrictor after fast there are  other women in his molars  this is a second dusk i was always very  interested in writing ever since i was a  kid  and for the longest time i thought that  writing had to be limited to a page and  to publications and  to books as a shy teenager she started  entering slam poetry competitions  and the words inside her started flowing  out  what i like about poetry is how  healing it can be it feels like a place  of complete liberated self-expression  it feels like a place where however  you're feeling that day or however  you're feeling in that moment can be  fully released i stand on stolen land  watch as flash flood and acid rain  embraces home  on the news a pig swims in the world  sees resilience  born in the philippines now living in  vancouver ivyadad was taking journalism  courses and decided to delve into  creative writing  event water towards mahala  the cost of a body is an empty home i  had to take a poetry class even though i  hated poetry at the time  um and then i dipped my teeth okay let's  rewind yeah  you actually hated poetry when you first  started creative writing and now you're  a poet  i never knew that i could have um  that i could have the mental capacity  for it the immediacy  of it is part of its charm part of  poetry's charm and power  kevin connolly is the poetry editor at  the house of a nancy  a publishing company he says poetry  plays an important role in times of  turbulence and unease  like during a pandemic i think in times  like this  people are at a loss for words i mean i  often ask people how they're doing  [Music]  people are having really awful time and  many people  whose life isn't all that changed and a  lot of them are at a loss for words they  can't quite  distill it and i think a poem can do  that because it's so specific to a  moment  uh a particular person's experience even  something that happened that day  he sees a surge of young poets  especially ones from marginalized  communities  these poets recognize the moment they're  in  some days i cannot see past the blood on  my hands  i read names like a eulogy the leopards  the gorillas the elephants the sea  turtles  the rhinos the tigers oh god how i had  never wished to outlive the tigers  when i first got into poetry it was i  was dealing with  a lot of mental health struggles and a  lot of my poetry focused on mental  health and focused on  you know moving through mental illness  and existing with mental illness  josek frequently writes about sexual  assault gender  issues and her personal struggles and  then  as i started to unpack more of my  healing i moved towards dealing with  survivorship and dealing with being you  know  a femme body in a society that is very  male focused in a society that  discourages  femininity and discourages the woman  for what are the boxes i encounter men  in but the packaging of their temptation  i am prayed into wrapping paper and  reused at every convenience  people write poetry out of a position of  anxiety they're much more comfortable  with anxiety than regular people  that's their strength and their weakness  but at times like this it seems like a  strength  edmonton was one of the first cities in  canada to formally establish a poet  laureate  in that role patel is the people's poet  your job as a poet laureate is to  reflect the times  and the thoughts and the feelings of the  people and a lot of my work as poet  laureate  has been in some ways more controversial  because it's critical  of the institutions around me she sure  isn't cow towing to the hand that feeds  her  here's her recent blistering attack on  city hall so i lay awake and wonder  which part of the municipal government  act  section 7 prevents the city from  spending money in a crisis  in the name of the safety health and  welfare of people  the fact patel is able to make a living  from her lyrics  has inspired this rising poet vancouver  is a demolished rainforest surrounded by  sacred mountains flattened for  skyscrapers surrey  is the pavement where mama tells me to  dream  poetry distills ideas down to a few  words  spread apart on a lot of white space no  wonder instagram has been a vehicle for  young poets  but critics of insta poetry describe it  as a few  superficial lines thrown together and  put into a square box  the spectator writes the vast majority  of instant poetry  is terrible cliched banal derivative  portentous repetitive and manipulative  i think you know young poets are not  always eloquent some of them  have difficult feelings complicated  feelings messy feelings right and to  capture that spirit in poetry even as  they grow older  i think is more human than some of these  old poets that are published in books  that we study for like hundreds of years  patel herself uses  instagram a platform she says helps  spread the word  but that also comes with its challenges  at the end of the day  i'm being put into this position where i  have to look at engagement numbers i  have to look at  whether a poem is landing in a in a good  way whether people are identifying with  it  and that part just it's like a worm that  gets into my brain and i'm really trying  to  reach a point with my writing where i'm  at peace where i can  offer what i want into the universe and  i don't have to accept  that social media metrics or how the  universe is telling me that what i'm  doing is right  connecting does depend on getting those  likes  chaucer and elliot had to worry about  followers and metrics  but it was a different era their works  have generally been relegated to the  musty corners of bookstores and  libraries but they're still taught by  the old establishment  do you think that poetry is still in  that realm of being traditional or  elitist or exclusive anymore  we're still preferencing people who go  to school for writing we're still  preferencing people  who sometimes have to write for 10 plus  years in order for them to be heard  they called me a coke nun brown on the  outside white  on the inside inside there is only 24  years  of hollowing yourself out patel didn't  pursue  publishing but a book deal has landed in  her lap  coconut is her debut to be filled by  someone else's expectations  so that you like a real coconut  can be judged in the palms of others and  i hope to  continue to build momentum i feel  momentous i feel like i'm caught up in  something  um that is bigger than me and i want to  see that through till the end whatever  the ending might be

